HYPOTHESIS  (DiscoveryBench — metadata_13)
Question: "In what centuries did we see the highest growth phase of the period between 2000 BCE and 1100 BCE?"

Headline answer
---------------
The highest growth phase in the 2000–1100 BCE window is the 15th century BCE (1500–1401 BCE),
with the 14th century BCE (1400–1301 BCE) forming a sustained extension of the same
expansion phase and the 13th century BCE (1300–1201 BCE) marking the maturity peak of
the resulting level of archaeological / economic activity.

Operationalization (column-based)
---------------------------------
- g_all_mean (Mean of KDE growth rates, time_series_data.csv): 15th BCE = +0.002829 (max
  in window, 100th-percentile), 14th BCE = +0.001451 (89th-percentile). The 15th–14th BCE
  pair is the only multi-century window where the KDE growth rate is positive and large;
  the next-lower century (16th BCE) drops to 0.001149, then collapses to ~0 or negative
  from the 13th century BCE onward.
- pollen_grate_100 (Percentage change of 100y-smoothed openness score, time_series_data.csv):
  15th BCE = +0.001889 (max in window), 14th BCE = +0.001114. These two centuries
  coincide with the largest positive change in pollen-inferred openness, i.e. the
  demographic / land-use "opening" of the landscape — strong independent confirmation of
  the same growth-phase identification.

Sustained-peak criterion (centuries within ~1 SD of the g_all_mean max):
- 15th BCE (0.00283), 14th BCE (0.00145), 16th BCE (0.00115). Same criterion on
  pollen_grate_100 returns: 15th, 14th, 17th century BCE — i.e., a sustained ~1500–1301 BCE
  growth band.

Corroborating magnitude proxies (level peak lags the growth-rate peak, as expected):
- kde_all_mean (level): 14th BCE = 0.000414 (max), 13th BCE = 0.000412 (2nd). Closely
  ranked with the growth-rate picks.
- kde_all_detrend (above-trend activity): 13th BCE = 0.000197 (max), 14th BCE = 0.000194.
  Right-shifted by exactly one century vs. the growth-rate peak.
- pollen_inter_100: 13th BCE = 2.039 (max), with 18th BCE a distant second.

Capital indicators (capital.csv Z-variables) — which dimensions drove the peak:
- 15th century BCE:  ZAxtSchwert (hatchets & swords) = +2.95 (max), ZHort (depot) = +1.04
  (max), ZMW (monument size) = +0.666 (max).  ⇒  violent/social-conflict capital and
  storage/integration peak together with the growth-rate high.
- 14th century BCE:  ZMonument (count) = +1.708 (max), ZBeil (axes/celt) = +0.56 (max);
  ZAmber = -0.01 (max). The "level" peak in monument counts and productive tools marks the
  consolidation of the growth phase.
- 13th century BCE:  composite capital mean|Z| = 1.203 (max within full centuries), driven
  by ZAusSchwert, ZDolch, Zhausgr (house size) and ZCu_Au (copper/gold) all sitting at
  elevated positive Z in this bin.

Partial-century caveat
----------------------
The 11th century BCE bin contains only the single year 1100 BCE (1 annual KDE row, 1
capital bin). It is therefore excluded from the ranking; its g_all_mean = -0.00534 and
pollen_grate_100 = -0.0026 are shrinkages from the 13th–12th BCE peak, i.e. the decline
that follows the 13th-century BCE maturity peak.

Concise answer
--------------
The 15th century BCE is the single century with the highest growth phase of the
2000–1100 BCE interval. The 14th century BCE forms a sustained extension of the same
growth phase (level peak). The 13th century BCE represents the maturity / consolidation
peak of capital indicators. This ranking is consistent across the headline growth-rate
proxy (g_all_mean), the independent pollen-based openness growth proxy (pollen_grate_100),
and is supported by the 100-y binned capital-Z indicators in capital.csv.
